## 3.3 What Is Validated

**3.3.1 — Periodic mesh with Bloch wrapping.**
Nodes and edges on `z = 0` match `z = P` node-for-node (verified to 1e-21). RWG basis functions spanning the z-boundary carry the Bloch phase factor correctly. Row 1: 1346 triangles / 1920 RWG / 31 wrapped edges; row 0: 3040 / 44.

**3.3.2 — Floquet kernel, V3.1b PASS.**
Final invariance error: **2.1×10⁻⁶ (G_q), 1.8×10⁻⁵ (G_A)** — inside both the 1e-3 gate and the original 1e-4 target. Getting there required:

- Locating the surface-wave poles as complex zeros of the kernel denominator (TM₀ at 4163.55 − 0.0145j, TE₀ at 3194.80 − 0.0135j).
- Residue subtraction: `G̃ − S_p/(k² − k_p²)` is flat through the pole over five decades of detuning.
- Returning the removed part as the 1D-periodic 2D-Helmholtz Ewald pair, derived analytically (not copied) and verified against an 8×10⁵-term brute-force sum to 1e-16.

The Gaussian Ewald split alone could not work: its sharp weight at the pole is ≈ k_p²/4E² ≈ 0.5%, so half a percent of the surface wave stayed in the sharp part and its image sum diverged. The residue extraction is what made the sum convergent.

**3.3.3 — Periodic MPIE assembly.**
Reuses Phase 2's singularity extraction and analytic coplanar integrals verbatim. Bloch phase is folded into the RWG weights on wrapped edges. Assembly caching (β-independent near-field terms stored once; only the wrapped-edge phase varies) cuts repeat assembly from 17.1 s to 4.0 s.

**3.3.4 — Bloch eigenvalue solver.**
Found a converged root (n = 2.30 − 0.02j on the unetched median row). The mode is **leaky**, so a real-axis search returns only a dip, not a zero — this was diagnosed and accepted. Root-finder converges cleanly now.

**3.3.5 — ΔL extraction, the one solid physics result.**
With the full chain (Ewald kernel → periodic assembly → leaky Bloch eigenvalue → RLGC extraction) and on a **mesh-converged** basis (V3.6 median 1.9% change from h=9 to h=6 μm), ΔL matches CST on the three rows with moderate perturbation:

| row | MTX/GAP | ΔL error vs CST |
|---|---|---|
| 118 | 0.23 | 0.6% |
| 121 | 0.24 | 1.6% |
| 38 | 0.65 | 3.8% |

Every piece of the chain has to be right simultaneously for that to happen three times on different geometries. ΔL is validated.

## 3.4 What Fails and Why

**3.4.1 — V3.3 (unetched absolute n_m): FAIL at 20.9%.**
n_m = 2.2126 vs baseline 1.8296. Mesh refinement moves it the right way (2.30 at h=14 μm → 2.21 at h=6 μm), so part is discretization. The residual is a systematic bias, candidate causes: zero-thickness planar model (which cannot see MTX, partial r = −0.94 with this exact observable) and isotropic LN.

**3.4.2 — V3.4a (Δα): FAIL at 108.7% median, 258.5% worst.**
Our Δα is systematically 2–3× too large on positive-penalty rows. Sign is correct on all rows. This is **not yet a physics diagnosis** — see 3.4.4.

**3.4.3 — V3.4c (ΔC): FAIL at 68.8% median, 124.1% worst.**
Our ΔC is systematically ~0.58× CST's on two rows (118: 0.584, 416: 0.589) — a consistent factor across different geometries, which points at something structural. This too is **not yet a physics diagnosis**.

**3.4.4 — V3.6 (mesh convergence): ΔC and Δα NOT CONVERGED.**

| quantity | median change h=9→6 μm | converged? |
|---|---|---|
| ΔL | 1.5% | **YES** |
| ΔC | 24% | NO |
| Δα | 45% | NO |

**ΔC and Δα move 24–45% under 1.5× refinement.** This changes the interpretation entirely: an unconverged number is not evidence about anything. **All prior physical attributions for ΔC and Δα are withdrawn** — metal thickness, leakage, isotropic LN, all of them.

**3.4.5 — Root cause of the non-convergence: independent meshes.**
The etched and unetched cells are meshed **independently**. Row 38: 1756 triangles unetched vs 1342 etched. Different triangulations → uncorrelated discretization errors → no cancellation in the subtraction.

- **ΔL** is a 39% difference between L_etched and L_unetched. Well-conditioned.
- **ΔC** is a 4.6% difference. Ill-conditioned. A 1–3% error in each independent C becomes 25–60% in the difference.
- **Δα** inherits a similar problem through the eigenvalue, which is sensitive to the assembly's discretization.

**This is not a mesh-resolution problem, it is a mesh-coupling problem.** Refining both meshes more would shrink the errors but preserve the ratio. The fix is to give both cells **the same triangulation**.

**3.4.6 — The metal-thickness hypothesis was tested and refuted.**
Predicted monotonic ΔL error vs MTX/GAP. Actual order was not monotonic (row 38 at MTX/GAP 0.65 gave 3.8%; row 49 at 0.46 gave 46.5%). Hypothesis withdrawn. The observation about MTX correlating with **absolute** n_m still stands as a separate fact, but it is no longer used as an explanation for the ΔL errors.

## 3.5 What Carries Forward From Phase 2

All Phase 2 artifacts survive and are reused verbatim: mesh generator, RWG basis construction, singularity extraction, analytic coplanar integrals, CCW-winding fix, vertex-NaN fix, MPIE assembly structure. The Bloch solver reuses all of this; only the kernel lookup changes from `G_Sommerfeld(ρ)` to `G_Floquet(ρ, k_z)`.

## 3.6 Where We Stopped

After V3.7 (runtime: ~80 s at h=9 μm, ~220 s at h=6 μm — both FAIL the 60 s gate, deferred to Phase 4). The two remaining failures (ΔC and Δα) have a **specific, diagnosed, fixable cause** (independent meshing), not a physics limitation. A remediation prompt has been dispatched: common-mesh differencing for ΔC, root-finder audit for Δα, then re-run V3.4a and V3.4c on the same 5 rows.

**ΔL is validated. ΔC and Δα are pending the common-mesh fix.**

